1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,800
Access modifiers taught us who may touch a
member. Package-private visibility only
makes sense if "same package" is a real
neighborhood, not an accident of whatever
folder someone happened to click.

2
00:00:11,919 --> 00:00:25,280
As a project grows, another problem
appears beside visibility: names collide,
and the filesystem stops matching what the
source claims. Two libraries can both
invent Utils. Two features can both invent
Order.

3
00:00:25,399 --> 00:00:37,640
Without namespaces, short names become a
fight. Onboarding suffers too: a newcomer
opens src and finds fifty classes in one
flat pile with no hint which ones form the
checkout story.

4
00:00:37,759 --> 00:00:47,560
So the natural question is: how does Java
give types an address space large enough
for a company, honest enough for the
compiler, and useful enough for access
boundaries?

5
00:00:47,739 --> 00:01:03,939
Packages are that geography — names,
folders, and boundaries together. Java's
common convention is reverse-DNS style
naming: com.shop.order, org.example.tools.
The point is uniqueness and hierarchy, not
fashion.

6
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:16,260
You own a domain, you flip it, you nest
features underneath. order alone is a
fight waiting to happen. com.shop.order is
an address. Look at this java for a
moment. The first line claims a package.

7
00:01:16,379 --> 00:01:29,739
On disk, this file should live under
folders that mirror com/shop/order. The
import brings User from another package
into short-name scope. Because the
packages differ, package-private members
of User stay hidden.

8
00:01:29,859 --> 00:01:39,019
Packages and access modifiers combine into
real boundaries, not just folder
cosmetics. Episode Three already
introduced packages as addresses for the
compiler.

9
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:48,340
Today the stakes are higher: packages are
how a growing product stays navigable, and
how "same package" remains a meaningful
friendship for package-private types.

10
00:01:48,459 --> 00:02:00,339
If everything sits in one package,
package-private collapses into "visible to
the whole app," and Episode Eleven's
careful ladder loses a rung. That
directory rule is the one that burns
beginners.

11
00:02:00,459 --> 00:02:14,579
The package declaration must match the
directory tree. If the file says
com.shop.order but sits in com/shop/orders
or directly under src, tools will fail to
resolve types, or you will compile one
layout and run another.

12
00:02:14,699 --> 00:02:22,299
Build tools like Maven and Gradle assume
this layout; fighting it means fighting
every tutorial and every teammate's muscle
memory.

13
00:02:22,419 --> 00:02:33,019
Once packages exist, you choose how to
name foreign types: import the short name,
or write the fully qualified name at the
use site. Look at this java for a moment.

14
00:02:33,139 --> 00:02:52,379
Fully qualified names are clear when two
types share a simple name — java.util.Date
versus java.sql.Date. Imports keep
ordinary code readable. Star imports —
import com.shop.user.*; — hide
dependencies.

15
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:03,860
A reader cannot see which types you
actually use, and a new class added to
that package can create ambiguous
references later. Prefer explicit imports;
let the IDE manage the list.

16
00:03:03,979 --> 00:03:17,379
What if the project ignores packaging
discipline? Giant catch-all packages
appear — com.shop.util, com.shop.misc —
where everything is package-private
friends with everything else.

17
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:25,219
Access modifiers stop meaning "module
interior." Folder structure drifts from
declarations. Onboarding becomes
archaeology.

18
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:40,020
A healthier shape groups types that change
together and hide together: Look at this
java for a moment. Outside com.shop.order,
callers see Order and its public methods.
They do not see OrderLine.

19
00:03:40,140 --> 00:03:51,740
That split is packages doing product work
— the same access ideas from Episode
Eleven, now with neighborhoods that make
those ideas enforceable. Access control
needed neighborhoods.

20
00:03:51,860 --> 00:04:05,020
Packages provide namespaces, map them to
directories, and give imports a way to
name foreign types. Combined with
modifiers, packages become boundaries —
and catch-all packages dissolve those
boundaries again.

21
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:15,380
Once types are organized, another modeling
need shows up for values that are not
free-form strings but a known set of named
constants — order status, ticket priority,
direction of a move.

22
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:25,740
You can fake that with magic ints and
string literals, and then every typo
becomes a runtime surprise the compiler
never saw. That pressure leads to enums.
